  <h2>How a turn feels</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cascadia</strong> is about as simple as turns get: take one of four tile-and-token pairs, add the
    tile to your landscape and place the animal on a matching spot. The thinking is spatial: grow your
    biggest habitat corridors while arranging bears, elk, salmon, hawks and foxes into the patterns this
    game's scoring cards reward.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Forest Shuffle</strong> asks you to take two cards or play one, paying with other cards from your
    hand. The paid cards go into the clearing for others to take. Each animal and plant has its own effect
    or scoring condition, so the puzzle is in the cards rather than in the layout.
  </p>

  <h2>How much there is to learn</h2>
  <p>
    Cascadia is the lighter of the two. Most people get it after one turn, and scoring follows five cards
    everyone can see. Forest Shuffle's turn is just as short, but there's more card text to read, and the
    final count takes longer because nearly every card scores differently. Neither is hard, but Cascadia
    is the one to bring to a table of people who don't usually play board games.
  </p>

  <h2>Replay value</h2>
  <p>
    Cascadia changes its scoring cards every game, so the same landscape-building asks for different shapes
    each time. Forest Shuffle's variety comes from the deck: which trees and animals show up, and what
    lands in the clearing. Its <a href="/games/forest-shuffle/forest-shuffle-expansions/">three expansions</a>
    add new species, bushes and caves with powers.
  </p>

  <h2>Two players and solo</h2>
  <p>
    Both work well at two. Cascadia includes a solo mode in the box. Forest Shuffle needs the Exploration
    expansion for solo play. At five players, Forest Shuffle is the one that fits: Cascadia goes up to four.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Upgrades for either one">
    Cascadia's cardboard nature tokens are a natural thing to upgrade: our
    <a href="/games/cascadia/">pinecone tokens and game tray</a> are made for it. For Forest Shuffle, the
    <a href="/products/forest-shuffle-insert/">insert</a> fits the base game and all three expansions, sleeved,
    in the original box.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The verdict</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Cascadia</strong> for families, mixed groups and short evenings: it's quick, calm and
    different every game. Choose <strong>Forest Shuffle</strong> if your group enjoys card combos and doesn't
    mind a longer count at the end, or if you play with five. If you love one, the other is different enough
    to own as well.
  </p>
